        <body>
            <div xml:id="d528">

                <pb facs="0645" n="1479" xml:id="pg1479"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/9696</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Netherland Chargé</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">De
                            Beaufort</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 23, 1918</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 25.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: I have the honor to
                    inform Your Excellency that the Royal Government has decided to send a convoy to
                    the Dutch East Indies to relieve the personnel of the Ministry, transfer the
                    Government officials and their families, and carry military supplies for the
                    colonial army.</p>
                <p>The convoy will consist of the following ships: The battleship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hertog Hendrik</hi> accompanied by a collier for coaling
                    during the voyage. A Dutch merchant vessel transformed into a warship in
                    accordance with the regulations of the seventh convention of 1907 which is to be
                    used in carrying soldiers and a cargo of military supplies. A Dutch merchant
                    vessel convoyed by the above-named battleship and carrying the Government
                    passengers, their families and merchandise belonging to the Government and
                    intended for the Government of the Indies.</p>
                <p>The shipment of merchandise and passengers will be closely watched by Government
                    officials. The baggage will also be examined, and private correspondence is
                    forbidden. The vessels will carry neither mail nor post parcels. Merchandise
                    will only be shipped when accompanied by certificates showing their origin in
                    accordance with existing arrangements.</p>
                <p>The route taken by the ships will be by the Scotch Islands and the Cape of Good
                    Hope. The sailing will take place in the early days of June next.</p>
                <p>Under instructions to inform the Government of the United States of the foregoing
                    I have the honor to have recourse to Your Excellency’s kindly good offices to
                    that effect.</p>
                <p>Be pleased to accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. de Beaufort</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
